Database.Batchable — Batch Apex

The interface for processing large record volumes by splitting work into chunks, each chunk running as its own transaction with its own governor limits.

apex
global class AccountCleanupBatch implements Database.Batchable<sObject> {

    global Database.QueryLocator start(Database.BatchableContext bc) { ... }
    global void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List<sObject> scope) { ... }
    global void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) { ... }
}

Invoked with Database.executeBatch(new AccountCleanupBatch(), 200), which returns the AsyncApexJob ID.

***********************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

The three methods


start()

Runs once, at the beginning. Its job is to define the total record set. Returns either a QueryLocator or an Iterable<sObject>.

apex
global Database.QueryLocator start(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
    return Database.getQueryLocator([
        SELECT Id, Name, Last_Activity__c
        FROM Account
        WHERE Sync_Status__c = 'Pending'
    ]);
}

The returned set is snapshotted at start time. Records that later stop matching the filter are still processed; records that newly match are not picked up. Because of this, execute() should re-verify preconditions rather than trusting the query filter.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
execute()

Runs once per chunk. Each invocation is a fresh transaction with a fresh set of governor limits — this is the whole point of batch.

apex
global void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List<Account> scope) {
    List<Account> toUpdate = new List<Account>();

    for (Account a : scope) {
        if (a.Sync_Status__c != 'Pending') continue;   // re-verify
        a.Sync_Status__c = 'Processed';
        toUpdate.add(a);
    }

    Database.update(toUpdate, false);   // partial success
}

Type the scope parameter concretely (List<Account>) rather than List<sObject> when you can — cleaner, and you skip the casting.

An uncaught exception in one execute() rolls back only that chunk. Other chunks are unaffected and the job continues, though the job status ends as "Completed with failures."

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

finish()

Runs once, after all chunks. Post-processing: send a summary email, chain the next job, update a control record.

apex
global void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
    AsyncApexJob job = [
        SELECT Id, Status, NumberOfErrors, JobItemsProcessed,
               TotalJobItems, CreatedBy.Email, ExtendedStatus
        FROM AsyncApexJob WHERE Id = :bc.getJobId()
    ];
    // notify, chain, log
}

