******************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

QueryLocator vs Iterable
	                                                                                  Database.QueryLocator                                                                                        	Iterable<sObject>
Record ceiling	                                                                        50 million	                                                                                 50,000 (counts against the normal SOQL row limit)
Source	                                                                             A single SOQL query	                                                                          Anything — API response, computed list, custom Iterator
Chunking	                                                                      Server-side, streamed via cursor	                                                                       Whole collection materialized in start()
Relationship subqueries	                                                               Not supported	                                                                                                  Supported
Default when	                                                                          Almost always	                                                                               Only when a plain query can't express the set

**********************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

QueryLocator is a server-side cursor. Salesforce never holds all 50M records in memory — it streams chunks. That's why the ceiling is so high and why it's the default choice.

Iterable exists for cases where the record set isn't a straight SOQL result:

global Iterable<sObject> start(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
    // Records derived from an external system, or from post-query filtering
    return new List<Account>(computeEligibleAccounts());
}

The 50,000 cap is the killer — you build everything in start(), so it's bound by the standard query-rows limit. If you find yourself reaching for Iterable because your criteria are too complex for SOQL, consider instead querying broadly with a QueryLocator and filtering inside execute().

For genuinely custom traversal, implement Iterator<sObject> and expose it through an Iterable:

apex
global class ChunkedIterable implements Iterable<sObject> {
    global Iterator<sObject> iterator() { return new ChunkedIterator(); }
}

********************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

Scope size

Second argument to executeBatch. Default 200, max 2,000.

Database.executeBatch(new MyBatch(), 50);

Sizing is a tradeoff between per-chunk limits and total chunk count:

Lower it (1–50) when each record does expensive work — callouts, complex DML cascades, heavy CPU. A callout-per-record batch with a scope of 100 will hit the 100-callout limit exactly; use 50 for headroom. Records with deep trigger logic often need 20–50.
Keep 200 for straightforward field updates.
Raise it (up to 2,000) rarely — for very light read-mostly processing where chunk overhead dominates.

Key detail: if a chunk fails on a governor limit, Salesforce may retry it with a smaller scope. But the chunk count itself is limited — total chunks across a job can't exceed the daily async limit, and a scope that's too small on a 50M-record job produces an absurd number of chunks.

Database.executeBatch with a QueryLocator ignores scope sizes above 2,000. With Iterable, scope behaves the same but you're already capped at 50k total.

