Database.Stateful

By default, instance variables reset between execute() calls. Each chunk gets a fresh deserialization of the class with only static final values preserved. Implement Database.Stateful to persist instance state across chunks:

global class RevenueRollupBatch implements Database.Batchable<sObject>, Database.Stateful {

    global Integer recordsProcessed = 0;
    global Decimal totalRevenue = 0;
    global List<String> errors = new List<String>();

    global void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List<Opportunity> scope) {
        for (Opportunity o : scope) {
            recordsProcessed++;
            totalRevenue += (o.Amount == null ? 0 : o.Amount);
        }
    }

    global void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        // Values survived all chunks
        System.debug(recordsProcessed + ' opps, total ' + totalRevenue);
    }
}

Caveats worth knowing:

Only instance variables persist. Statics still reset.
State is serialized between chunks, so it counts against heap and everything held must be serializable. Don't accumulate large collections — a List<String> of errors across 50M records will blow up. Cap it, or write to a custom object instead.
Stateful batches can run slower, since serialization happens between every chunk.
Common legitimate uses: running counters, aggregation totals, a bounded error list, an ID captured in start() that finish() needs.

*******************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

Chaining batches

finish() is the only place you can start another batch job from within a batch.

global void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
    AsyncApexJob job = [
        SELECT NumberOfErrors, JobItemsProcessed, TotalJobItems
        FROM AsyncApexJob WHERE Id = :bc.getJobId()
    ];

    if (job.NumberOfErrors == 0) {
        Database.executeBatch(new AccountRollupBatch(), 200);
    } else {
        notifyFailure(job);
    }
}

Rules and constraints:

Only one batch can be chained from finish() in practice — you can call executeBatch but you're bound by the 5-concurrent-batch-jobs limit and the 100-jobs-in-flex-queue limit.
Chaining from execute() is not allowed.
finish() can also enqueue a Queueable or schedule a job — often cleaner for lightweight follow-up work.
Developer/Trial orgs cap chain depth at 5. Production is unbounded, which means a bug that chains unconditionally will run forever. Always gate the chain on a terminating condition.

A guard pattern for iterative chains:

global class RecursiveBatch implements Database.Batchable<sObject>, Database.Stateful {

    private Integer depth;

    global RecursiveBatch(Integer depth) { this.depth = depth; }

    global void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        Integer remaining = [SELECT COUNT() FROM Account WHERE Needs_Processing__c = true];

        if (remaining > 0 && depth < 10) {
            Database.executeBatch(new RecursiveBatch(depth + 1), 200);
        }
    }
}

Two termination conditions — work remaining and a hard depth cap. The depth cap is the one that saves you when the "work remaining" query has a bug.

