Testing

@IsTest
static void processesAllRecords() {
    List<Account> accts = new List<Account>();
    for (Integer i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
        accts.add(new Account(Name = 'Test ' + i, Sync_Status__c = 'Pending'));
    }
    insert accts;

    Test.startTest();
    Database.executeBatch(new AccountCleanupBatch(), 200);
    Test.stopTest();       // batch runs to completion here

    System.assertEquals(200, [
        SELECT COUNT() FROM Account WHERE Sync_Status__c = 'Processed'
    ]);
}

The critical constraint: only one execute() invocation runs in a test context regardless of scope size. Keep test data under one chunk, or your assertions will be wrong. To exercise multi-chunk behavior — particularly Database.Stateful accumulation — call execute() directly with hand-built scopes:

MyBatch b = new MyBatch();
b.execute(null, chunkOne);
b.execute(null, chunkTwo);
System.assertEquals(400, b.recordsProcessed);

********************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

Choosing between async types
	Use when
@future	Fire-and-forget, mixed DML split, callout from trigger. Legacy default.
Queueable	Non-primitive params, need a job ID, need chaining. Modern default for most async.
Batch	Record volume exceeds what one transaction can handle. The volume tool.
Schedulable	Time-based trigger; typically just kicks off a batch.

If the record count is bounded and modest, Queueable is simpler. Batch earns its complexity once you're past roughly 10,000 records or need the per-chunk limit reset



